@* ----------------------------------------------------------------
    AlertAnalysis view -- component version (Major.Minor.Build.Revision)
    Version: 1.0.55.175
    1.0.55.175 - BOOKMARK FILTER. A "Bookmarked" toggle beside aaViewTog narrows BOTH views to the
               operator's bookmarked alerts (aaState.bm, applied in fnAaFiltered like every other
               facet, so Table and Timeline always agree). The button carries the bookmarked count in
               the current scope. It stays DISABLED until GetMyBookmarks answers: filtering on an
               unloaded set would show "no bookmarks" when the truth is "unknown". Removing a bookmark
               while the filter is on re-renders, so the alert leaves the list once the server confirms.
               Clear filters resets it.
    1.0.55.174 - ALERT BOOKMARKS. A bookmark button sits beside the Failure/Predictive chip on the
               timeline card, the lane detail, the table Type cell and the alert modal badge. Saved per
               operator through AlertAnalysis/ToggleBookmark (controller 1.0.6.7), which stamps the
               user server-side -- the browser sends only alertId / siteId / target state. The target
               state is EXPLICIT (bookmarked true/false), never "flip", so a double click or a retry
               cannot land the opposite of what the operator sees. Until GetMyBookmarks answers, the
               buttons are UNKNOWN (dimmed), not "off": an unreachable service must not read as
               "nothing bookmarked". Toggle is optimistic and reverts with a message on failure.
    1.0.55.173 - DEFAULT ENGINE = QUANTUM (DeepSeek Pro) FOR ALL. Owner decision supersedes .66/.172:
               _aiProvider hard default 'quantum11' -> 'quantum10'; aaApplySiteKeepGate now forces
               'quantum10' for non-privileged sessions (was quantum11); the privileged dropdown
               'selected' moved to the QUANTUM option. The server maps quantum10 -> deepseek ->
               DeepSeekModel (deepseek-v4-pro). Pairs with controller 1.0.160.202. Pro is the slower
               model; QUANTUM 1.1 (Flash) stays selectable for privileged users.
    1.0.55.172 - DROPDOWN DEFAULT MATCHES THE ENGINE. The privileged model <select> carried
               selected on nexus10 while _aiProvider has been hard-set to 'quantum11' since .66
               (H3) and nothing on init reads the select back -- so the page SHOWED NEXUS and RAN
               QUANTUM 1.1 (the open UI/session mismatch from the .159 review). 'selected' moved
               to the quantum11 option; display-only, no behaviour change (fnAiSetProvider is
               still the only writer of _aiProvider). Pairs with controller 1.0.160.201, which
               makes quantum11 the server-side default for provider-less callers too.
    1.0.55.171 - DUPLICATE UNIT FIX. Two spots still doubled a unit that the metric name already
               carried (e.g. "Ir mA mA"). (1) The "How the fault developed" eyebrow (both render
               paths) concatenated the raw unit -- now routed through metricUnitSuffix() like the
               numbers card and compact hero. (2) metricUnitSuffix() hardened: it now also de-dups a
               trailing qualifier "RG mA (avg)" + "mA" and a value glued to its unit "110V" + "V",
               while STILL handling the parenthesised-unit case "VTC TFC O/P(V)" + "V" (no regression).
               Note: "Ir mA mA" on THE NUMBERS card was already de-duped since .158 -- a live sighting
               there means a stale deployed build; and "Ir" showing at all on derived track causes is
               the separate server-side viz-anchor bug, not this view fix.
    1.0.55.170 - PM ANALYSIS SCROLL, RE-APPLIED. This was written once already, against a tree
               that was never merged back, and it was numbered .169 -- the same number the rating
               work then took on the .168 base. Two different .169s existed and this tree had the
               rating one, so the panel still could not scroll. It is .170 here; if a file
               labelled .169 ever turns up carrying the scroll and no Edit button, that is the
               orphan, not a newer build.
               WHAT IT FIXES: the panel is one story card, the deck fixes its height at paint,
               and the card clips its overflow (.161, to stop wide plots crossing into Live
               data), so anything growing AFTER paint -- the result box filling on a click -- was
               cut off at the old height. #aiPmBody is now capped and scrolls internally, which
               keeps the deck height steady whatever is clicked. A click scrolls the panel the
               minimum needed to reveal the result box and no more, so the tile stays under the
               pointer; scrollIntoView is deliberately not used, it recentres.
    1.0.55.169 - RATING AND REMARK ARE NOW EDITABLE, and the remark holds 2000 characters.
               EDIT: the .163-.164 overlay was write-once by design and said so on its face
               ("Ratings cannot be changed once given"). That note is gone because it is no
               longer true. A rating that is YOURS gets an Edit button which reopens the form
               pre-filled with your stars and your remark; saving goes through the same Upsert,
               which is an upsert, so the row is updated rather than duplicated. A rating that
               belongs to SOMEONE ELSE is never editable from here -- the overlay shows it
               read-only and offers "Add my rating" instead, which files your own row under your
               own name. The server stamps identity (.164), so an operator still cannot rate or
               edit as anybody else; this only decides which button the overlay draws.
               Cancel out of an edit and the stored rating is untouched, including after a failed
               save -- a failed edit leaves the form open with the text still in it and says NOT
               SAVED, same rule the first save has followed since .163.
               2000 CHARACTERS: maxlength 500 -> 2000 on the textarea, with a live counter, and
               the server rejects anything longer instead of letting the upstream truncate it
               silently. NOTE: this is the limit THIS page enforces. If the rating service's
               Comment column is narrower than 2000, the upstream will truncate or 500 and no
               view change can prevent it -- worth confirming before operators rely on the space.
    1.0.55.168 - CLICKING A CLASS TILE MOVED THE TILE. Tile clicks wrote their result into
               #aiPm4Sel, which sits inside the TIMELINE card near the top of the panel. That box
               starts empty, so the first click inserted a couple of lines ABOVE the Class
               Timeline and pushed everything below it down -- the tile slid out from under the
               pointer. The Class Timeline now has its own box, #aiPm4ClsSel, directly beneath the
               strips, so a tile reports where it was clicked. Both boxes are also rendered with
               their prompt text and a reserved min-height from the start, so filling either one
               no longer changes the height of anything.
               Same run: pm4SelectTile built its label with a literal "&middot;" and pm4ShowOp
               escapes the label, so the entity was showing as text -- the .160 fault repeated one
               function over. It passes the character now, not the entity.
    1.0.55.167 - RATING RESTRICTED TO SITE-KEEPING USERS. The test is window.AA_SITEKEEP, the flag
               .55.63 already resolves server-side, and it sits inside aiRateBtnSync rather than
               in aaApplySiteKeepGate's hide list: that gate runs once at ready(), while
               aiRateBtnSync runs on every alert switch and sets display='' again, so the id-list
               route would have hidden the button and then un-hidden it -- the same fault .55.44
               records against Re-run. fnAiRateOpen carries the same test, since a hidden button
               is not an access control. NOTE: AA_SITEKEEP is IsSiteKeeping OR a match in
               SiteKeepingOverrideEmails (.55.63), so the config-listed accounts can rate too; if
               rating must key on IsSiteKeeping ALONE, that is a separate flag to emit.
    1.0.55.166 - THE REMARK BOX COULD NOT BE TYPED INTO. The overlay was appended to <body>, which
               puts it OUTSIDE the open Bootstrap modal, and the modal's focus trap
               (enforceFocus, the reason #aaAlertModal carries tabindex="-1") drags focus back
               to itself whenever focus lands on anything that is not one of its descendants.
               The textarea took clicks and never took focus, so keystrokes went nowhere. It is
               now appended inside #aaAlertModal. I copied the <body> attach from Tool trace,
               where it is harmless because that overlay has no fields -- a pattern being proven
               elsewhere is not proof for a use it was never put to.
    1.0.55.165 - RATE POPUP DID NOT OPEN: "wEsc is not defined". The .163 rating code borrowed
               wEsc/wTok/wErrMsg from the Wisdom helpers, which I had read at their definition
               without checking their SCOPE -- they live inside an IIFE in a separate <script>
               element, so they are private to it and were never globals. Nothing hoists across
               script elements or out of a closure, so the very first render threw and the
               overlay stayed blank. Escaping now goes through escapeHtml(), which is top-level
               in this same block, and the token and error helpers are defined inside the rating
               block itself. The rating code now depends on nothing outside its own block except
               escapeHtml and the alert state it is built on.
    1.0.55.164 - RATING IS NOW READ BACK. .163 kept the rated state in a page variable only, so an
               F5 wiped it: the operator reopened an alert that was already rated, saw an empty
               star row, and a second submit would silently overwrite the first through Upsert.
               The overlay now asks AIRating/Get for the analysis before it offers a form, via
               the same same-origin proxy for the same reason as the post. Three states, not two:
               RATED (read-only, as the .163 screenshot), NOT RATED (form), and UNKNOWN -- if the
               lookup fails the form is NOT shown, because an unreachable service is not evidence
               that nobody has rated this, and treating it as such is precisely how the first
               rating gets overwritten. The lookup also drives the footer button, so it reads
               "Rated" before the overlay is ever opened. The API returns an ARRAY; the row is
               picked server-side (the caller's own row if present, otherwise the most recently
               updated) so the view never has to guess which of several is THE rating.
    1.0.55.163 - RATE THIS ANALYSIS. Re-instates the outcome signal .101 removed, as a 1-5 star
               rating with an optional remark instead of the .98 valid/not-valid pair. Three
               things had to be true before a control could be shown again. (1) The row id must
               belong to the OPEN alert: the `analysis_id` event has been landing in a bare
               window._aiAnalysisId since .98, and fnSetAiAlertContext never cleared it the way
               .55.29 clears the run id -- analyse A, open B, rate, and the rating is filed
               against A. It is now keyed by alert in _aiAnalysisIdByAlert and re-read on every
               alert switch. (2) The post goes through a same-origin action on
               AlertAnalysisController, never at the rating host directly: that host is plain
               http on an internal name, which an https page cannot call and a client machine
               cannot resolve -- the wall .157 hit with PM Analysis. Identity is stamped
               server-side from LoginUser, so a rating cannot be filed under another name.
               (3) A failed post says NOT SAVED and leaves the stars editable. .101's rule was
               that a visible control which cannot save is worse than no control; the button is
               therefore HIDDEN entirely until a row id for the open alert exists, which also
               means a REUSED verdict is unrateable unless the controller emits analysis_id on
               its cache-serve path. Overlay reuses the rvk chrome Tool trace already uses.
    1.0.55.162 - MERGE of the PM Analysis work into this tree. This file carried the PM code at the
               .157 state (separate #aiPm4View, legacy compact renderer, band/tile-strip class
               code) while its version header already said .161, so none of .158-.162 was actually
               present -- which is why the graphs did not appear. Merged in whole: the chapter
               binding (.158), the api/pm4/analyze parameters and window/cache meta (.159), the
               entity-escaping fix and on-demand Plotly loading (.160), explicit plot widths and
               overflow containment (.161), and the Class Timeline stroke-shape tiles (.162).
               Everything outside the PM Analysis regions in this file is untouched.
    1.0.55.161 - PREDICTIVE shown as FAILURE. The alert-card badge used "PREDICTIVE if type starts
                 with PRED, else FAILURE" -- so a blank or variant alertType (some rows arrive with
                 alertType empty) mislabelled a predictive alert as FAILURE. Now detects BOTH
                 directions explicitly, falls back to the alert DESCRIPTION ("predictive Alert" /
                 "failure Alert") and then AlertCategoryId (1=pred, 2=fail), failing to FAILURE only
                 when nothing indicates predictive. Owner: "predictive alert shows failure".
