            1.0.55.155 - TWO count/timeline corrections (owner).
            (1) COUNTS NOW FOLLOW THE SELECTED SITE. The header spills (Predictive/Failure/Total), the
            Asset-type chips (TRACK/SIGNAL/IPS/POINT MACHINE) and the All/Failure/Predictive segment
            all counted over AA_INRANGE -- the whole date window, every site -- so picking a site tile
            never changed them. They now count over aaScope(): AA_INRANGE narrowed to aaState.site when
            one is chosen, the whole window otherwise. SITE ONLY -- the segment and the type chips are
            themselves the other facets, so they must not self-narrow. The cause-mix pie follows the
            site too. No site selected = unchanged (aaScope returns AA_INRANGE). AA_TOTF/AA_TOTP stay
            window totals, so the ALL SITES tile still rolls up every site.
            (2) TIMELINE SHOWED FEWER ALERTS THAN THE TABLE. The day lane collapsed a burst (same site,
            within 20 min) into ONE card with a count, so a 5-alert burst read as one mark -- fewer
            than the table's 5 rows for the identical filtered set. That clustering was a leftover from
            the pre-.85 true-time-position layout, where cards at nearby times overlapped; the .85
            rebuild put cards in sequential FLOW, so nothing can collide and the clustering only hid
            alerts. Now ONE CARD PER ALERT, so the timeline and the table show the same set.
            1.0.55.112 - MAINTENANCE PLAN CARDS in the follow-up. Renders the same [[CARD]] block the
            ChatBot uses (1.2.9.5) -- one marker format, one look, whichever surface the plan was
            asked on. Priority pill and left border by URGENT/INSPECT SOON/MONITOR, label-value rows,
            emphasised action, muted note, single column under 560px. Cards are lifted out BEFORE the
            markdown pass and spliced back after, so neither pass can damage the other; an answer
            without markers is untouched.
            1.0.55.111 - FOLLOW-UP ANSWERS NOW RENDER. The follow-up chat printed the model's reply with
            escapeHtml() alone, so every "##" heading, "**bold**" and "- bullet" appeared on screen as
            literal characters -- a structured answer read as one flat wall of text. New aiChatMd()
            renders headings, rules, ordered/unordered lists, inline code, fenced blocks, pipe tables
            and bold/italic, with escaping FIRST so nothing the model emits can inject markup. Live
            streaming still uses plain textContent (it is written token by token); the finished answer
            is re-rendered when done() calls renderAiChat(). User turns stay plain -- they are the
            operator's own words. Styling follows the popup's diagnostic-card language.
            1.0.55.110 - GREETING header (controller .162, owner option D). When the server sends a
            'greeting' event (logged-in sessions, flag on) a warm one-line banner shows at the top of
            the analysing panel -- time-of-day + first name, then what the tool is doing. It updates
            in place when the asset-history synopsis arrives after prefetch. window._aiGreeting is
            reset at the start of each run so a prior alert's line never carries over. Pure header:
            nothing about the verdict path changes.
            1.0.55.109 - LIVE ALERT EVENTS. Subscribes to the broadcaster at
            {AlertEventsWsBase}/subscribe/alertEvents/{siteId} for every site on the board (one
            socket per site, capped at 12, reconciled against AA_SITES inside fnAaDerive so filter
            changes open/close only the delta). An incoming event raises a toast -- asset, site,
            cause, Failure/Predictive tone -- with an Analyze button, and schedules ONE silent
            board reload 4 s later however many events arrive. Reconnect backs off 3s->30s;
            visibilitychange reconciles after sleep; events are de-duped by alertId for 90 s.
            NOT auto-analysing: a just-fired alert has not settled, so an automatic verdict would
            be formed on incomplete evidence and then cached (the .153 lesson) -- the human presses
            Analyze. The message parser is deliberately tolerant (bare object, array, or
            data/payload/alert/body envelope; field names tried in order) because the frame shape
            is not contractually known here; an unrecognised frame still refreshes the board and
            logs itself to the console for one-time shape confirmation.
            1.0.55.108 - Run log button REMOVED from the popup footer (owner: "remove run log option,
            still showing deepseek"). The panel listed per-run engine/model; the .159 server fix
            redacts that feed to calling names, but the owner wants the surface gone entirely.
            wisdomOpenRuns() is left dormant (unreachable); the RunLogFeed endpoint stays, gated
            and .159-redacted, for LogWatch/ops use by direct URL.
            1.0.55.107 - Asset-history card background WHITE (owner: the .106 purple gradient read
            dull/muddy behind the donut). Clean #fff card, purple hairline border kept, soft
            elevation shadow added. Everything on the card was already tuned for a light ground
            (white slice separators, white hole, purple inks) so this is one CSS rule.
            1.0.55.106 - GLOSS pass on the asset-history donut (owner: dull, wants impact + glossy).
            Per-slice light-to-deep SVG linear gradients, a radial sheen highlight clipped to the
            upper arc of the disc, white slice separators, a raised WHITE hole with hairline ring
            and heavier centre figure, glossy share bars (gradient fill + inner highlight) and
            swatches, gradient card background, deeper drop shadow, gradient accent strips.
            Gradient/clip ids salted per render. View-only; payload unchanged.
            1.0.55.105 - Asset history chapter restyled -- the .103 flat pie read dull. Now a DONUT with
            the total centred inside it, card-colour separator strokes between slices, a soft drop
            shadow, legend rows with per-cause share bars and tabular counts, the window span under
            the donut, and the same-cause recurrence as an accented strip (purple; green "first
