            1.0.55.104 - REUSED badge shows the badge, the time and nothing else. The engine/model segment
            is removed unconditionally (owner: "model never display -- just cache/fresh"): the
            controller stopped sending cached_engine in .155, but rows CACHED before .155 carry the
            field inside their copied payload forever, so the view must ignore it even when present.
            1.0.55.103 - ASSET HISTORY as a new story chapter. Pie of the asset's cause-code spread over
            the controller lookback (verdict.asset_history, AiChat .154 Layer A) -- pure-SVG slices
            largest-first from 12 o'clock, legend with count and percent, and a sub-line with the
            total/span and the same-cause recurrence with its last reset latency. Renders only when
            the controller sends the block (flag ON and fetch succeeded); REUSED opens replay it
            because the block travels inside the stored verdict.
            1.0.55.102 - REUSED badge date fixed. cached_at was truncated with substring(0,16), written for
            ISO input, but the controller sent a locale string ("8/15/2026 8:47:35 PM") -- the badge
            showed "8/15/2026 8:47:3". New fmtCachedAt() parses the value (ISO from controller .153,
            legacy locale strings from older rows) and renders HH:mm:ss/dd.MM.yyyy, falling back to
            the raw string. Applied to the badge and to the Export-PDF reuse line. The cached_engine
            segment is unchanged here -- the controller now sends that field only to site-keeping
            sessions, and this line already renders it only when present.
            1.0.55.101 - Removed the "Was this alert valid?" buttons. The endpoint was moved out of
            AiChatController (it is a UI action, not part of the AI flow) and the owner paused the
            work before it was rebuilt in AlertAnalysisController -- leaving buttons that posted to
            a route which no longer exists. A visible control that cannot save is worse than no
            control: the operator believes a judgement was recorded when it was not. The SSE
            `analysis_id` event stays, so re-adding the feature needs only the endpoint and the bar.
            1.0.55.100 - Train mark redrawn as a VANDE BHARAT trainset. The steam-style loco with a chimney
            read as dated and, at 40px, as a lorry. A Vande Bharat is recognisable from its
            silhouette alone: a low sloped nose, a continuous window band running the length of the
            set, a roof line unbroken by a chimney or a cab box, and no separate locomotive -- it is
            a single articulated unit, which is exactly what distinguishes it from a loco-and-coach
            drawing. Two cars, blue window band on white, bogies in pairs under the car ends.
            1.0.55.99 - ASSET HEALTH moved out of the pre-verdict slot and into THE STORY. It sat ABOVE the
            verdict, so the first thing a reader met on a CONFIRMED fault was a green "HEALTHY"
            band -- two green ticks arguing with a red verdict before the verdict had been read.
            That placement was mine (Cut F), chosen so a critical standing condition could not hide
            in the caveats. The fix for hiding it was never to put it first: it is a SEPARATE,
            LONGER-RUNNING fact about the asset, and it belongs beside the other chapters where a
            reader goes for context. A CRITICAL condition still surfaces above the verdict, because
            that one genuinely outranks a single alert.
            1.0.55.98 - WAS THIS ALERT VALID? Two buttons under the verdict, posting to the audit API via the
            controller. This is the first outcome signal the system captures: until now nothing
            recorded whether a verdict was right, so accuracy was an impression. Deliberately a
            plain question about the ALERT, not a rating of the AI -- an operator knows whether the
            fault was real, and that is the fact worth storing. The choice is shown as taken and can
            be changed; a failed post says so rather than looking recorded.
            1.0.55.97 - REFRESH dropped average and MinFail. aiRefreshLive rebuilds each row from the live
            feed and carries the safe range across from the analysis snapshot -- but I listed the
            fields explicitly and omitted avg and minFail, so after one Refresh the average tick,
            the fail marker and the whole "6% below normal" wording vanished. The card silently
            degraded to less than it had shown a second earlier. Now carried, and the merge copies
            the reference row rather than re-listing its fields, so a field added later cannot be
            lost the same way again.
            1.0.55.96 - LIVE DATA legibility. The bar carried three unlabelled markers -- value, average,
            MinFail -- and a first-time reader had no way to tell which was which. Each tile now
            states the RELATION in words: "at normal", "6% below normal", "LOW - below 250". A
            marker position only means something to someone who already knows the design; the words
            do not need explaining. Train mark redrawn as a clearer glyph.
            1.0.55.95 - THE STORED VERDICT WAS NEVER USED. The "Analyze" button called fnAnalyzeAlert(TRUE) --
            the same argument Re-run uses -- so every first analysis declared itself a re-run and the
            server bypassed its cache. Every diagnostic in the 15 Aug logs carries "force": true, and
            that is why: none of them COULD have been served from store. Cut G (verdict persistence,
            version floor, reset rules) and G3 (the AI column on the alert list) were both working
            and unreachable, because the client asked for a fresh run every time. Analyze now sends
            FALSE; only Re-run sends true.
            1.0.55.94 - The train graphic read as a TRUCK: a tall box with a smaller box in FRONT and two
            wheels, which is a cab-over lorry. Redrawn as a locomotive -- long low body, a raised
            cab at the REAR, a chimney, a headlamp at the front, a buffer beam, and three small
            bogie wheels rather than two large road wheels. Cab behind body and wheels in a bogie
            group are what separate a loco from a lorry at 40px. A coach is coupled behind it so the
            silhouette reads as a train rather than a single vehicle. Pairs with controller
            1.0.160.136, which fixes the station-code regression that stopped this card appearing.
            1.0.55.93 - The position bar showed only where a value sits between MinSafe and MaxSafe. It could
            not show where NORMAL is inside that band, so a reading well below its own average still
            looked mid-range. The bar now carries an AVERAGE tick and, where one exists, a MinFail
            marker -- MinSafe and MinFail are different thresholds and only one was visible. On S-46
            the band is 90-150 V with an average of 109.3; a 95 V reading is inside the band and far
            below normal, which the bar can now say. Pairs with controller 1.0.160.135.
            1.0.55.92 - LIVE DATA card redesigned. It was a flat list of eleven rows in which nothing stood
            out -- the reader had to compare each value against its own range to find the one that
            mattered. Now each reading is a small tile with a POSITION BAR showing where the value
            sits between MinSafe and MaxSafe, so "in range" is read at a glance from the marker and
            only the exceptions need reading. Out-of-range tiles are tinted and sorted FIRST, stale
            channels carry an age chip, and the header states the exception count and the oldest
            reading rather than repeating the timestamp.
            1.0.55.91 - Refresh now sends the RUN ID and ALERT ID with the LiveValues request, so its server
            log line can be joined to the AI diagnostic for the same run. Without them a failure
            landed in a different log with only a timestamp to match on -- the line that explains a
            blank card was there and unfindable. Pairs with AlertAnalysis 1.0.6.6.
            1.0.55.90 - "Still active" was STILL wrong after 55.88, because both of the client's sources for
            resetTime are optional and both can be absent when the popup opens. The controller now
            sends it (1.0.160.130) from the FRS alert row it already fetched, so the view merges one
            authoritative value instead of guessing from two.
            LIVE DATA returned nothing: I parsed CurrentValue / AssetAttributeName / MinValue /
            MaxValue, and the DataAPI LiveValue response carries NONE of them -- it returns Value,
            AssetAttributeId and TimestampDevice. Every row failed the numeric parse and was skipped,
            so the grid was empty on every click. Names and safe ranges now come from the snapshot
            already held, matched by attribute id.
            1.0.55.89 - The current-values grid sat between the identity strip and the verdict, in a place
